<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sogdians</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sogdian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sogdians were an Iranian people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, famed as merchants along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. They served as traders, diplomats and administrators for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Gokturk Empire</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>. Accounts of the Tang Dynasty note their commercial and military roles, including in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan-rebellion">An Lushan Rebellion</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sogdians.jpg" alt="Sogdians" width="320" height="152" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A.Davey from Portland, Oregon, EE UU · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sogdian_Tribute_Bearers_on_the_Apadana_Staircase_16_(Best_Viewed_Size_%22Large%22)_(4689076272).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Sogdia, or Sogdiana, lay between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>, including the fertile valley of the Zeravshan, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> urban culture appears at Sarazm in Tajikistan, as early as the 4th millennium BCE. The Sogdian city-states, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, were never politically united. Sogdian is an Eastern Iranian language, no longer spoken, although Yaghnobi, a descendant of one of its dialects, is still spoken by the Yaghnobis of Tajikistan. Sogdiana was a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, named on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription">Behistun Inscription</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> conquered it in 546 to 539 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s forces captured the Sogdian Rock in 327 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roxana">Roxana</a>, daughter of the Sogdian nobleman Oxyartes, married Alexander. Sogdia later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom</a>, the Sakas and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> conquered it in 260 CE.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a> took it around 479, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-turkic-khaganate">First Turkic Khaganate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> defeated them perhaps in 557, after which the Turks kept the land north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a>. Qutayba ibn Muslim began the Muslim conquest of Sogdia in the early 8th century, and Sogdians, aided by the Turgesh, expelled the Umayyad garrison from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>.</p><p>Conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was virtually complete by the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a> in 999, and Sogdian was largely supplanted by New Persian. Genghis Khan later destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, but in 1370 Samarkand revived as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Sogdians turned their energies to trade so thoroughly that the Saka of Khotan called all merchants suli, Sogdian. They learned trade from the Kushans and became the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> middlemen after the Kushan collapse, establishing a trading network across the 1,500 miles from Sogdiana to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. In 568 a Sogdian diplomat, Maniah, persuaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istami">Istämi</a>, ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-turkic-khaganate">First Turkic Khaganate</a>, to send an embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-ii">Justin II</a> agreed to an alliance against Sasanian Persia and a direct <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> trade.</p><p>Other Sogdians settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> for generations and rose to prominence in the military and government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>. They first practised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and, to a lesser extent, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a>. The 6th century is thought to be the peak of Sogdian culture, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrasiab">Afrasiab</a> paintings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> and the murals of Panjakent are rare surviving examples of its art.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sogdia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Sogdia, which they conquered in 479 or 509 and which grew extremely prosperous as the centre of a new Silk Road linking China with the Sasanian and Byzantine empires; the Hephthalites took over the Kushans' old role as intermediary and contracted <b>Sogdians</b> to carry silk and luxury goods.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…to make the Uyghurs leave Luoyang, in 780 Tun Baga Tarkhan demanded 1,800,000 strings of cash in gold and silk as compensation for Uyghurs and <b>Sogdians</b> killed leaving Chang'an, and in 822 the Tang paid 70,000 pieces of silk to send home troops whose help they had declined.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uyghur-khaganate">Uyghur Khaganate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The same roads carried the faiths of Iran: <b>Sogdian</b> merchants brought their fire temples to the Chinese capitals and, as the Manichaeans faded in the West, carried Mani's faith east.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li></ul></section>
